@X0BREQUIREMENTS-
-------------

@X03To install PCBoard, you'll need at least 530k conventional memory free.  To
make sure that you meet this requirement:

@X091) Open a dos session.  If you're already in dos, exit any multitasker you may
   be using.  From the dos command prompt, type
   @X07mem /c | more
   @X09This will provide page breaks.  Now, look at the line that says @X03Largest
   Executable Program Size:@X09.  This will indicate the memory that we're focusing
   on.  If it is less than 530k, then you'll need to disable some drivers, or
   load them into upper memory until you have 530k free.  The programs listed
   above are those that are already loaded.  It will tell you if they're in
   conventional memory and how much space they take.

@X03You'll encounter problems during the installation process as soon as you run
out of memory.  So it may be a memory problem even if the install program loads
successfully at first.
 
You'll also need a 386 or higher PC to install the program.  If you're using a
286 or slower machine, contact voice support at CDC (1-801-261-1686) for
details on how to install on a 286.
 
@X0B
PROCEDURE-
----------
 
@X03When you first run the install program, it will ask you for a drive and
directory to install pcboard in.  The default directory is \PCB.  You will
find that keeping your installation consistent with the defaults will avoid
future problems and complications.  If there is a problem with \PCB or you
have another preference, enter it here.
 
Next, you are prompted for your first name.  Second, your last name.  Third,
your city and State.  Lastly, a password.  These are the name and password
that will be used to create your sysop login record for the BBS.
@X04This CAN be changed later.@X03
 
At a later point, it will prompt you for the components to install.  If this is
your first installation, you MUST install a @X0BFIRST TIME INSTALL@X03.  Press the
space bar to select it.  Now, select the additional (optional) components you'd
like installed.  This may include @X0BCOMMDRV@X03, @X0BPPLC@X03, or @X0BOS/2 FILES@X03.  If you're
running OS/2, you'll need OS/2 FILES, and you will not need COMMDRV.  If you
have PPLC, you'll want to install that as well.  PPLC will be an option even
if you do not have PPLC.  If you are not sure, you can select to install it,
and it will not cause any problems.  When you are finished installing, you can
check by looking for @X0CPPLC.EXE @X03in the \PCB directory.
 
Do *NOT* select @X0BUPGRADE INSTALL@X03 if this is your first time installation.
 
If this is not your first time install, you are upgrading, or need to install
certain components to your machine, then you'll need to select @X0BUPGRADE INSTALL@X03.
This will tell the install program to install whatever you like, but NOT the
initial install files and .EXE programs.  They will not install unless you also
select UPGRADE INSTALL.
 
Now you'll see pcboard shell to a text browser and show you SAMPLES.DOC.  This
document will allow you to read about the different kind of boards that PCBoard
can install.  When you're finished browsing, press the ESC key to go on.
Next, It will ask you for the type of BBS that you would like install.  If you
want ALL features installed, you will want to select a Hobby Board.  With all
features installed, you have the option to disable those that you don't want.

It will prompt you to tell it to update your autoexec.bat.  You'll want to
select <Y>es.  This will put the \PCB directory in your path.  If you tell it
no, then it will not update it, and you will have to update it manually.  It
will next probmpt you to update your config.sys file.  If you do not tell it it
update your config.sys file, you'll need to manually do it later.
 
 
@X0B
 
SETUP-
-------

@X03The PCBsetup program is now run to allow you to make any immediate necessary
changes to your BBS.  The only necessary change (under DOS, Windows 95,
Windows 3.1)is @X0BPCBSETUP | NODE CONFIGURATION | RUNNING A NETWORK/MULTITASKER
SYSTEM <y/n>?  @X03You'll need to select no, or run share before loading PCBoard.
 
Upon completion of the execution of PCBsetup, the setup procedure continues
to run the pcbmodem program.  This program will test your modem for response
and also reset the NRAM settings for the modem.

First you must select your make of modem.  Next, the model.  NOTE:  If you have
a 28.8 kbps modem and it is not listed, look for v.34.  This is the protocol
that is capable of giving modems their 28.8 speed.  This will be the
appropriate setting.

Second, you'll need to enter a portspeed.  This can be confusing for some
people.  If you only have a 28.8 modem, how can you open the com port at 57600?
If you set your portspeed to 38400, 57600, or  115,200, then they'll all be
faster than 28.8, right?  well, the portspeed is bottlenecked by your modem.
Most serial cards are capable of 115.200 kbps.  You'll want to set it higher
than your maximum modem speed, but not TOO much higher.  You'll find that
having a 1200 baud modem connect to a 115,200 connection is a difference of
114000 bps.  That's a lot of speed to negotiate and sometimes you can't
connect that way.  It is recommended that you set the openign portspeed as
close to the modem's maximum baud rate as possible, and still keep it higher
so that it's not handicapped.
 
It will ask you for your comm driver to use.  If you are using dos and have
a fossil interface installed, Select F.
 
If you have COMMDRV (COMMTSR) installed, then you'll want to select C.  Note
that this is only valid in a dos environment.
 
If you are running under OS/2, select O.  This is regardless of the PCBoard
version, the serial driver, the fossil driver, the serial card, or any other
circumstances.  If you're under OS/2, select O.
 
If you are using no multitaskers, no GUIs, no multiport serial boards, and
are running under a straight dos session, then select A for direct UART
communication.
 
Next, you'll be prompted for the com port.  This is what determines which modem
is to be initialized.  If you select com2, then the modem connected to com2 will
be initialized.  This change is unique to the modem, not the com port.
